<h3 id=3D"heading-steps-to-download-and-set-up-git-bash-on-windows" class=
=3D"g-md-h3 gamut-t98mq7-StyledText e8i0p5k0">Steps to download and set up =
Git Bash on Windows</h3>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">1. Navigate to the <a target=3D"_blank" href=3D"https:/=
/git-for-windows.github.io/" class=3D"g-md-a gamut-1r92bn9-AnchorBase e14vp=
v2g0" rel=3D"noopener">Git Bash installation page</a> and click the Downloa=
d button.=20
<img src=3D"https://content.AI Academy by RiWoT.com/courses/freelance-1/unit-3/git%2=
0bash%20setup/annotated_gitbash_dl.png" alt=3D"download" class=3D"g-md-img"=
>
2. Once Git Bash is downloaded, run the downloaded <strong class=3D"g-md-st=
rong">.exe</strong> file and allow the application to make changes to your =
PC. You will get a prompt that says, =E2=80=9CDo you want to allow this app=
 to make changes to your device?=E2=80=9D Click <strong class=3D"g-md-stron=
g">Yes</strong>.
<br class=3D"g-md-br"> <br class=3D"g-md-br">
3. To keep things simple, we will use the default settings for everything i=
n this installation, so all you need to do now is keep clicking <strong cla=
ss=3D"g-md-strong">Next</strong>, and finally <strong class=3D"g-md-strong"=
>Finish</strong>.
<img src=3D"https://content.AI Academy by RiWoT.com/courses/freelance-1/unit-3/git%2=
0bash%20setup/annotated_gitbash_installer.png" alt=3D"setup" class=3D"g-md-=
img">
4. Open the Start menu by clicking on the Windows icon and typing =E2=80=9C=
Git Bash=E2=80=9D into the search bar.  The icon for Git Bash and the words=
 =E2=80=9CGit Bash Desktop App=E2=80=9D will appear. Click on the icon or t=
he words =E2=80=9CGit Bash Desktop App=E2=80=9D to open Git Bash.
<img src=3D"https://content.AI Academy by RiWoT.com/courses/freelance-1/unit-3/git%2=
0bash%20setup/annotated_gitbash_start.png" alt=3D"openGitBash" class=3D"g-m=
d-img">
5. A new window will open. This is the Git Bash CLI where we will run Bash =
commands. Whenever a new window of the Git Bash app is opened, you will alw=
ays be placed in the same directory, your <strong class=3D"g-md-strong">hom=
e directory</strong>. </p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">The home directory is represented by the tilde sign, <c=
ode class=3D"g-md-code">~</code>, in the CLI after <code class=3D"g-md-code=
">MINGW64</code>. The tilde is another way to say <code class=3D"g-md-code"=
>/c/Users/username</code> in Git Bash or  <code class=3D"g-md-code">C:\home=
\Users\username</code> in Windows=E2=80=99 Command Prompt. </p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">The absolute path of your current working directory, ho=
w you got from the root directory to the directory you are currently in, wi=
ll always be noted at the top of the window:</p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p"><img src=3D"https://content.AI Academy by RiWoT.com/courses/free=
lance-1/unit-3/git%20bash%20setup/annotated_gitbash_shell_edited.png" alt=
=3D"homeDirectory" class=3D"g-md-img"></p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Git Bash works by giving you a CLI that acts like a Bas=
h CLI. That means you can now work with your files and folders using Bash c=
ommands instead of Windows commands.</p>
<p class=3D"g-md-p">Congratulations, you now have Bash installed on your co=
mputer, ready to use!
In case you wanted to try out the built-in Windows command line for basic s=
ystem tasks, we talk about it in <a data-testid=3D"domain-agnostic-link" hr=
ef=3D"https://www.AI Academy by RiWoT.com/courses/learn-the-command-line/articles/wi=
ndows-command-line" target=3D"_blank" class=3D"gamut-1r92bn9-AnchorBase e14=
vpv2g0">this article</a>.</p>
